  fullWidth={true}
>
  <div data-hero-page>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="relative h-[45vh] max-h-[450px] min-h-[300px] flex items-center justify-center">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-navy">
        <img
          src="/Images/stock/1581092160607-ee22621dd758.webp"
          alt="Thermal imaging camera pointed at commercial building infrastructure showing heat differential patterns"
          class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-25"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-[rgba(11,31,58,0.82)]"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="relative z-10 text-center px-6 max-w-[760px] mx-auto">
        <p class="label text-gold mb-4">INSIGHTS</p>
        <h1 class="text-[1.8rem] md:text-[2.6rem] font-light text-offwhite leading-tight">
          Thermal Imaging in Commercial Property &mdash; What It Actually Reveals
        </h1>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Article Body -->
    <article class="bg-white section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-[760px] mx-auto px-6">

        <!-- Introduction -->
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            Thermal imaging has been used in building diagnostics for decades, but most property owners and construction professionals still think of it as a niche tool &mdash; something you bring in when you suspect a specific problem. A leaking roof, maybe. An overheating electrical panel.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, calibrated thermal capture reveals far more than most people realise. And when it's integrated into a structured site documentation programme rather than used as a standalone inspection tool, it becomes one of the most valuable data layers in the entire survey.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a practical guide to what thermal imaging actually shows in commercial buildings &mdash; and why it belongs in every serious site survey, not just the ones where something has already gone wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- How Thermal Imaging Works -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">How Thermal Imaging Works in Buildings</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            A thermal camera doesn't see heat. It sees infrared radiation &mdash; energy emitted by every object based on its surface temperature. The camera converts these radiation patterns into a visual temperature map, where differences of fractions of a degree become visible as colour gradients.
          </p>
          <p>
            In a building context, this matters because temperature differences on surfaces are caused by what's happening behind, above, or within those surfaces. A warm patch on a ceiling tile reveals an active HVAC duct behind it. A cool stripe down a wall indicates a hidden water pipe. An anomalous hotspot on an electrical panel door suggests a connection running above its rated load.
          </p>
          <p>
            The camera doesn't diagnose. It reveals. And what it reveals is often invisible to every other form of inspection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Moisture Detection -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">Moisture Detection &mdash; The Most Valuable Application</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            Water damage in commercial buildings extends far beyond what's visually apparent. A roof leak might show as a stain on a ceiling tile directly below the penetration point &mdash; but the moisture itself migrates through the building assembly, travelling along purlins, through insulation, and across vapour barriers before it appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thermal imaging detects this migration because evaporating moisture cools the surface it's evaporating from. A thermal camera scanned across a ceiling, wall, or floor will show moisture-affected areas as measurably cooler patches against the ambient surface temperature. The pattern and extent of these cool zones map the actual moisture footprint &mdash; which is almost always larger than the visible damage.
          </p>
          <p>
            This has significant implications for two audiences in particular:
          </p>
          <p>
            For <a href="/services/insurance-loss-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">insurance loss documentation</a>, thermal imaging provides an objective, timestamped record of moisture extent at the time of capture. This matters because moisture migrates over hours and days &mdash; what you document on day one after a loss event will be different from what exists on day five. Early thermal capture establishes the baseline that adjusters, restoration contractors, and engineers all work from. Without it, moisture extent becomes a matter of opinion rather than evidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            For pre-construction and <a href="/services/construction-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">conditions surveys</a>, thermal capture identifies moisture issues that visual inspection misses entirely. A ceiling that looks dry may have active moisture migration above it. A wall that appears sound may have concealed moisture behind the finish. These are the conditions that produce mould, structural deterioration, and expensive mid-project discoveries if they're not identified before work begins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Electrical Infrastructure -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">Electrical Infrastructure &mdash; Hotspots That Prevent Failures</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            Electrical distribution equipment generates heat at every connection point. Under normal conditions, this heat is distributed evenly and within acceptable limits. When a connection degrades &mdash; due to corrosion, loosening, overloading, or component deterioration &mdash; resistance increases at that point, and localised heating follows.
          </p>
          <p>
            A thermal camera pointed at an electrical panel, a busbar, a transformer, or a motor drive will show these hotspots clearly. The temperature differential between a normal connection and a failing one can be 20, 40, or 80+ degrees above ambient &mdash; and the connection will look perfectly normal to the naked eye until the day it fails.
          </p>
          <p>
            In <a href="/services/industrial-facility-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">industrial and manufacturing facilities</a>, thermal scanning of electrical distribution and motor drives is a standard element of predictive maintenance programmes. But it's equally valuable as part of facility documentation &mdash; creating a thermal baseline that identifies existing issues and provides a reference point for future comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            In <a href="/services/data-centre-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">data centre environments</a>, where electrical reliability is existential, thermal documentation of the power chain from utility intake through UPS and PDUs to the rack level provides a spatial record of thermal conditions across the entire distribution system. Georeferenced within the 3D model, each thermal observation is tied to a precise location &mdash; not a photograph with a vague label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Building Envelope -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">Building Envelope &mdash; What the Walls Aren't Telling You</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            The thermal performance of a building envelope &mdash; its ability to maintain a temperature differential between inside and outside &mdash; is largely invisible. You can't see missing insulation. You can't see a thermal bridge at a steel column. You can't see air infiltration at a window frame junction. But a thermal camera, used under the right conditions, can see all of these.
          </p>
          <p>
            Envelope thermal imaging is most effective when there's a meaningful temperature differential between interior and exterior &mdash; typically 10&deg;C or more. Under these conditions, areas of poor thermal performance show clearly as anomalous temperature zones on interior surfaces. Missing or displaced insulation appears as cold patches. Thermal bridges &mdash; where structural elements conduct heat through the envelope &mdash; appear as linear temperature drops. Air infiltration at junctions and penetrations shows as localised cooling patterns.
          </p>
          <p>
            For building owners and operators, this data informs energy performance assessment, capital planning for envelope upgrades, and identification of areas prone to condensation and moisture problems. For construction teams undertaking renovation or refurbishment, it identifies existing envelope conditions that will affect the design and specification of the new work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Solar and Renewable Energy -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">Solar and Renewable Energy &mdash; Panel-Level Diagnostics at Scale</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            Thermal imaging applied to photovoltaic arrays operates on the same principle &mdash; temperature differentials reveal performance problems &mdash; but at a completely different scale.
          </p>
          <p>
            In a <a href="/services/solar-farm-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">solar farm environment</a>, drone-mounted thermal cameras capture thermal data across arrays spanning dozens or hundreds of acres. Underperforming panels show as anomalously warm compared to their neighbours. Specific failure modes produce characteristic thermal signatures: hotspot cells from bypass diode failures, string-level anomalies from wiring issues, and uniform temperature elevation from soiling or degradation.
          </p>
          <p>
            When this thermal data is georeferenced &mdash; pinned to precise panel locations within the array model &mdash; it transforms from a thermal image into an actionable maintenance schedule. The operations and maintenance team receives a list of specific panels at specific locations with specific anomaly types, rather than a heat map of a field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Thermal Image vs Thermal Documentation -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">The Difference Between a Thermal Image and Thermal Documentation</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            This distinction matters, because it's the difference between useful data and data theatre.
          </p>
          <p>
            Anyone can point a thermal camera at a wall and produce a colourful image. Social media is full of them. But a thermal image without context &mdash; without calibration data, without emissivity settings documented, without spatial reference, without ambient conditions recorded &mdash; is an illustration, not evidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            Professional thermal documentation means the capture is calibrated (emissivity set correctly for the material being measured), the ambient conditions are recorded (air temperature, humidity, wind conditions for exterior work), the observations are spatially referenced (tied to a specific location in a 3D model or floor plan), and the findings are integrated into a structured report that your engineering team or consultants can act on.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why thermal imaging works best as an integrated layer within a comprehensive <a href="/services/pre-construction-site-intelligence/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">site documentation programme</a>, not as a standalone service. The thermal data gains meaning from the spatial, visual, and written documentation around it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- When to Include Thermal Imaging -->
        <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mt-16 mb-6">When to Include Thermal Imaging in a Site Survey</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
          <p>
            Thermal imaging adds value to almost every commercial site survey, but it's most consequential in these scenarios:
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
          <div class="border-l-2 border-gold pl-6">
            <h3 class="text-navy font-medium">Before any renovation or fit-out</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-midgrey text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed">
              To identify concealed moisture, <a href="/insights/above-ceiling-mep-survey-fit-out/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">above-ceiling MEP routing</a>, insulation deficiencies, and electrical issues before design is committed.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-gold pl-6">
            <h3 class="text-navy font-medium">After a loss event</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-midgrey text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed">
              To document moisture extent and damage scope while conditions are still fresh. The <a href="/services/insurance-loss-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">evidence window closes quickly</a> and thermal data captured early is materially different from thermal data captured a week later.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-gold pl-6">
            <h3 class="text-navy font-medium">For facility condition assessments</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-midgrey text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed">
              Particularly across portfolios where consistent thermal data enables like-for-like comparison between buildings. <a href="/services/education-campus-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">Education campuses</a> and <a href="/services/self-storage-portfolio-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">self-storage portfolios</a> benefit from this approach.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-l-2 border-gold pl-6">
            <h3 class="text-navy font-medium">For ongoing monitoring</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-midgrey text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed">
              Annual or semi-annual thermal capture creates a longitudinal record that reveals developing problems before they become emergencies. Electrical hotspots that are 15&deg;C above ambient this year and 25&deg;C above ambient next year are telling you something.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Conclusion -->
        <div class="mt-16 pt-16 border-t border-border">
          <h2 class="text-[1.6rem] font-light text-navy mb-6">Conclusion</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-midgrey leading-body text-[1.05rem]">
            <p>
              Thermal imaging isn't a gimmick and it isn't a luxury. It's a data layer that reveals conditions invisible to every other form of inspection. When it's calibrated, spatially referenced, and integrated into structured documentation, it transforms a site survey from a visual record into a diagnostic one.
            </p>
            <p>
              The question isn't whether to include thermal capture in your next site survey. The question is what you'll miss if you don't.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Footer note -->
        <p class="mt-12 text-[0.85rem] text-midgrey/60 italic">
          AlturaScope integrates FLIR thermal imaging into every survey where it adds value &mdash; from single-site pre-construction documentation to multi-site portfolio programmes across North America and the UK. <a href="/contact/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">Tell us about your project</a>.
        </p>

        <!-- Internal Links -->
        <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-border flex flex-wrap gap-6 text-sm">
          <a href="/services/insurance-loss-documentation/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">Insurance loss documentation &rarr;</a>
